<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Hehe Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1855 CE – 1898 CE (approx.) · capital Kalenga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A warrior kingdom of southern Tanzania whose chief Mkwawa annihilated a German column in 1891 and resisted colonial conquest for seven years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the mid-19th century the chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munyigumba">Munyigumba</a> united the many small Hehe chiefdoms of the southern highlands by conquest and alliance, adopting military methods learned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> who had invaded the region. After his death in 1879 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mkwawa">Mkwawa</a> won a succession struggle and built the Hehe into the dominant power of the area.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Hehe state was organised for war, with regiments of spearmen and a capital at the fortified town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalenga">Kalenga</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mkwawa">Mkwawa</a> raided neighbours, controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to the coast and demanded tribute from traders. On 17 August 1891 his warriors destroyed a German column under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emil-von-zelewski">Emil von Zelewski</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lugalo">Lugalo</a>, one of the worst colonial defeats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Germans stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalenga">Kalenga</a> in 1894, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mkwawa">Mkwawa</a> waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war until 1898, when, cornered, he shot himself. His skull was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania</a> in 1954. The Hehe lands became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1855 CE – 1898 CE (approx.